00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.030
So, stay tuned.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:23.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.800
Shared VPC provides the
ability for organizations

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.830
to connect resources from
multiple service projects

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.290
to a common VPC.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.630
This is amazing as it
provides centralized control

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.110
over networking resources, like
subnets, routes, and firewall

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:40.800
rules.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.980
And it was great for
VMs with a single NIC,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.570
but it wasn't ideal for VMs
which require a NIC 0 to be

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.220
a non-shared VPC network.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.980
This means something like
this was not supported--

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.670
NIC 0 would be tied
to the local VPC

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.910
while NIC 1 would be
assigned to the shared VPC.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.470
To support this,
customers would have

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.190
to deploy proxies to
front-end the instances, which

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.880
led to more management
and resource overhead.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.150
With this recent launch,
we now support shared VPC

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.250
on a non-default NIC.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.420
Now, you can attach a shared
VPC to any NIC of an instance.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.730
And this eliminates the
requirement of proxies

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:25.160
and gives more flexibility
for VM configurations.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.710
And, now, on to MTU.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.920
MTU stands for Maximum
Transmission Unit,

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.990
the size of the
largest protocol data

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.930
unit that can be communicated
in a single network layer

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.160
transaction.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.710
A larger MTU can bring
greater efficiency

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.380
because each network
packet carries more data

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.220
while protocol overheads,
like headers, remain fixed.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.020
1,500 bytes MTU is an industry
standard IP packet size.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:55.550
The default MTU for a Google
Cloud VPC is 1,460 bytes.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.620
As of the end of
2020, you're now

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:01.290
able to increase the
MTU to 1,500 bytes.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.850
While this gave VMs the ability
to exchange 1,500 byte IP

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.070
packets with other
VMs, services,

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.620
or the public internet,
there was still

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.410
a discrepancy with Cloud
Interconnect, which

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.450
provides direct
physical connections

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.720
between your on-premise
network and Google's network.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.930
Prior to this launch, Google
Cloud Interconnect MTU

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.120
was set to 1,440 bytes.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.810
With this new launch, we now
support end-to-end 1,500-byte

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.430
MTU support.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:32.030
Organizations can lift and shift
or support hybrid workloads

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.250
with strict MTU requirements.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.440
And that's what's
new in networking

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.480
for core infrastructure.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.090
Let us know in the comments if
you think these will accelerate

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.980
and improve your Cloud journey.

